"[1512.01736v1] A $K$-quadrilateral cosine characterization of Aleksandrov spaces of curvature bounded above (https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.01736v1)\nciteturn0view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.01736v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Metric Geometry\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 6 Dec 2015]\nL9: # Title:A $K$-quadrilateral cosine characterization of Aleksandrov spaces of curvature bounded above\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†I.D. Berg , cite6†Igor G. Nikolaev L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled A $K$-quadrilateral cosine characterization of Aleksandrov spaces of curvature bounded above, by I.D. Berg and Igor G. Nikolaev\nL14: \nL15: cite7†View PDF cite8†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:In this note, we extend the main results of our paper on quasilinearization and curvature of Aleksandrov spaces of curvature $\\leq0$ to curvature bounds other than $0$. For non-zero $K$, we employ the previously introduced notion of the $K$-quadrilateral cosine, which is the cosine under parallel transport in model $K$-space, and which is denoted by $\\operatorname{cosq}_{K}$.\nL17: Our principal result states that a geodesically connected metric space (of diameter not greater than $\\pi/\\left(2\\sqrt{K}\\right) $ if $K>0$) is an $\\Re_{K}$ domain (otherwise known as a $\\operatorname{CAT}\\left(K\\right) $ space) if and only if always $\\operatorname{cosq}_{K}\\leq1$ or always $\\operatorname{cosq}_{K}$ $\\geq-1$. (We prove that in such spaces always $\\operatorname{cosq}_{K}\\leq1$ is equivalent to always $\\operatorname{cosq}% _{K}$ $\\geq-1$).\nL18: As a corollary, we give necessary and sufficient conditions for a Cauchy complete semimetric space to be a complete $\\Re_{K}$ domain. We show that in our theorem the diameter hypothesis for positive $K$ is sharp and we prove an extremal theorem when $|\\operatorname{cosq}_{K}|$ attains an upper bound of $1$. We derive from our main theorem and our previous result for $K=0$ a complete solution of Gromov's curvature problem in the context of Aleksandrov spaces of curvature bounded above.\nL19: Then we establish the $K$-Euler's inequality and the extremal theorem for equality in the $K$-Euler's inequality in an $\\Re_{K}$ domain.\nL20: Subjects:  | Metric Geometry (math.MG)\nL21: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 53C20 (Primary) 53C45, 51K10 (Secondary)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:1512.01736 [math.MG]\nL23:    | (or cite10†arXiv:1512.01736v1 [math.MG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1512.01736†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Igor Nikolaev [cite12†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Sun, 6 Dec 2015 04:45:58 UTC (270 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled A $K$-quadrilateral cosine characterization of Aleksandrov spaces of curvature bounded above, by I.D. Berg and Igor G. Nikolaev\nL35: \nL36:   * cite7†View PDF L37:   * cite8†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite13†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite14†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: math.MG\nL44: \nL45: cite15†< prev |   cite16†next > L46: \nL47: cite17†new | cite18†recent | cite19†2015-12 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite20†math L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite21†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite22†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite23†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Bu